course: reflection
lesson: 15
title: The lie nobody found out: carrying a secret
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A lie or a wrong thing that nobody ever finds out does not vanish: it moves inside you and sits there, and it often pops up at night or whenever someone says the wrong word.
explain: Last summer Leo told his mum he'd lost his phone, but really he'd swapped it with a mate for a game; Mum paid for a new one and cried about the money, and months later, in bed, he still thought, "She cried over money because of me. And I just let her."
explain: At first he felt clever, then sick whenever money came up, especially when Mum said "we can't afford it"; getting away with it felt worse than being caught, because he carried it alone, and the weight did not fade by itself.
explain: When he finally came clean, crying, Mum was upset, then hugged him and said "thank you for telling me", and he paid some of it back from his birthday money; telling the truth late was still far better than never.
explain: The guilt is your conscience working, not proof that you are a bad person; doing a wrong thing means you need to fix it and change, and owning up is usually the first step that makes the weight lighter.
fact: A lie that is never found out does not disappear; it often stays inside and can feel worse than being caught, because you carry it alone.
fact: "Come clean" means to tell the truth about something you hid; telling the truth late is still better than never.
fact: Guilt is the conscience working; it is a sign you know what is right, not proof you are a bad person.
fact: Taking something from a shop without paying is stealing (shoplifting); going back with a grown-up to pay and say sorry helps put it right.
fact: Letting someone else take the blame for what you did is unfair to them; owning up later is still brave.
fact: Saying mean things about someone behind their back, and being "two-faced" (nice to their face, mean behind their back), is a kind of bullying; a real sorry and changed behaviour matter most.
fact: Praise for copied work does not feel real, and copying stops a teacher from seeing what you need help with.
fact: Most parents are less upset about an accident than about it being hidden; hiding makes guilt grow.
fact: Hurting an animal is wrong; doing something cruel when angry means the anger needs help, and grown-ups can teach better ways to deal with it.
fact: Reading someone's private things, like a diary, breaks trust; admitting it is hard but right.
fact: Things gained by stealing rarely make people happy, because guilt spoils them.
fact: Laughing along when someone is bullied, out of fear, is common; making up for it and standing up next time is possible.
exercise: Leo lied about losing his phone and nobody found out. Months later he still thinks about it at night. Why? | A hidden lie stays inside and is carried alone | Lies that aren't found out always disappear within a week | He is worried only because he might still get caught | It means he has a bad memory for other things
exercise: What did Mum say when Leo finally came clean? | "Thank you for telling me," after being upset | That she would never trust him again in his life | That it didn't matter because it was so long ago | Nothing; she pretended she hadn't heard him
exercise: Ruby took sweets from a shop without paying. Nobody saw. What helped? | Telling Mum, who went with her to pay the shop and say sorry | Avoiding the shop for the rest of her life | Taking something back to the shop secretly, without saying anything | Telling herself that nobody got hurt
exercise: Sam broke a window and his friend got blamed. Sam stayed quiet. What is true? | Letting someone take the blame is unfair; owning up is brave | Owning up late is pointless, so he should stay quiet | His friend should have proved he didn't do it | It's fine, because his friend wasn't badly punished
exercise: Mia said horrible things about a girl behind her back, and the girl found out and cried. What helped? | A real sorry and being kind to the girl afterwards | Saying "everyone says stuff" to make it seem smaller than it was | Avoiding the girl for the rest of the year | Saying the girl must have misheard
exercise: What does "two-faced" mean? | Nice to someone's face, mean behind their back | Having two different best friends at the same time | Changing your mind about what you like and don't like | Being honest with everyone, even with total strangers
exercise: Ethan copied homework and the teacher praised him. He feels like a fraud. What helped? | Telling the teacher and asking for extra help | Copying again so that his marks stay high all year | Making his own work look worse on purpose next time | Telling his friend to stop doing the homework for him
exercise: Ava broke Mum's favourite vase and hid the pieces in the bin. What is true? | Most parents are less upset about an accident than about the hiding | Hiding things always makes the guilt go away faster | Mum will never notice, so there's no problem | Broken things are always the parent's fault
exercise: Noah kicked a cat in the street because he was angry about something else. What is true? | Hurting an animal is wrong, and his anger needs help | It doesn't matter because it was just a cat | He is evil and can never change | The cat must have done something first to deserve being kicked
exercise: What helped Noah? | Telling Dad, who taught him better ways with anger | Trying hard to forget it ever happened and telling nobody | Kicking a ball instead every time forever | Blaming the cat for being in the way
exercise: Lily read her sister's diary and now knows a secret she can't "unknow". What helped? | Saying sorry to her sister for reading it | Pretending to everyone she never read anything | Telling the secret to her friends at school | Reading the diary again to understand it better
exercise: Kai took money from his dad's wallet to buy a game. Every time he plays it, he feels bad. Why? | Guilt spoils things that are got by stealing | The game is just not very good | His dad is watching him play every single time he plays it | Games always feel bad after a week
exercise: What did Kai do to put it right? | Told Dad and paid it back from pocket money | Played the game even more to forget the guilt | Sold the game quietly and kept all the money | Put the money back secretly without saying anything
exercise: Isla laughed when her friend was bullied, because she was scared of the bullies. What is true? | Laughing along out of fear is common, and she can make up for it | She is now exactly as bad as the bullies forever | It doesn't matter because she didn't start it | She should keep laughing to stay safe
test: Leo's mate goes, "if nobody found out, you got away with it, job done, bruv." What's the truth? | The lie stays inside, and carrying it alone is worse | He's right; a lie nobody finds out does no harm to anyone | He's right, as long as you never think about it again | Lies only matter if a teacher finds out
test: Ruby's cousin, Aussie: "Nah, nicking a few lollies from the servo doesn't count, mate." ("Nicking" means stealing; "lollies" are sweets; a "servo" is a petrol station.) What's right? | It's still stealing; paying and saying sorry helps | It doesn't count if it's only sweets and nobody gets hurt | It only counts if a camera sees you | It's fine if the shop is very big
test: What would happen if Sam never owned up about the window? | His friend would stay wrongly blamed and Sam would keep the guilt | His friend would forget it and Sam would feel fine | The window would be fixed faster | Nothing at all would happen to anyone
test: Mia says, "I'm a bully. I'm just a bad person." Which thought is more useful? | I did a mean thing; saying sorry and changing is what matters | I'm bad, so there is no point in trying to change anything now | Everyone says stuff, so it doesn't count | It was the girl's fault for finding out
test: In a cartoon, a kid copies homework, gets a gold star and feels great forever. What's unrealistic? | Praise for copied work usually doesn't feel real | Nothing; copying always feels great when you get a star | Teachers never notice any copying at all in real life | Gold stars make every bit of the guilt disappear for good
test: Ava's gran, Scottish: "Ach, hen, a broken vase is nae bother, it's the hidin' that's the trouble." ("Hen" means dear; "nae" means no.) What does she mean? | The accident is small; hiding it is what makes things worse | Breaking vases is fine, but only for grandmothers, not children | Ava should hide things better next time | Vases are never important to anyone
test: Noah's mate: "it's just a cat, chill, it ran off." What's the honest view? | Hurting an animal is wrong, and his anger needs help, not hiding | His mate is right; animals don't really feel pain like people do | It's fine if the animal runs away afterwards | Cats don't count because they're not people
test: Lily's sister finds out her diary was read. Lily says, "I was just being nosy, it's not that deep." What would be better? | A real sorry, because reading it broke her trust | Nothing; nosy people can't help it, so there's nothing to say sorry for | Promising to only read it on weekends | Telling her sister to hide it better
test: Kai's mate: "you got the game, didn't you? W." ("W" means a win.) Why doesn't it feel like a win to Kai? | Guilt about the stolen money spoils the game | The game was too easy to finish | His mate wanted the game for himself | He doesn't like that kind of game
test: Isla thinks, "I laughed, so I'm as bad as the bullies forever." What's a fairer thought? | I laughed out of fear; I can say sorry and stand up next time | I'm exactly as bad as them now, so nothing I do can ever change it | It doesn't matter because I didn't start it | My friend should have laughed too
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a falsehood which remains undiscovered? | It commonly persists within the mind of its author as a burden borne alone | It ceases to have any effect upon its author within a short period | It is of consequence only upon its discovery by an adult | It invariably brings satisfaction to its author
exam: It is given that a pupil allowed another to bear the blame for his own act. Determine the course of action consistent with integrity. | Acknowledgement of responsibility, notwithstanding the delay | Continued silence, since the matter has been concluded | Insistence that the blamed pupil prove his innocence | Concealment pending the eventual discovery of the truth by others
exam: Identify the principal reason for which most parents respond more gravely to concealment than to the accident concealed. | Concealment damages trust, whereas an accident is not deliberate | Accidents are invariably more costly than concealment | Parents are customarily indifferent to accidents of every kind whatsoever | Concealment is the only matter subject to legal penalty
exam: Ascertain which statement is consistent with the facts concerning guilt experienced after wrongdoing. | It is the operation of conscience and not proof of irredeemable character | It establishes that the person is permanently and wholly bad | It is a disorder requiring the suppression of all memory of the wrongdoing | It arises only where the wrongdoing has been discovered
